  pages   = {576--601},
  year    = {2023},
  doi     = {10.1214/23-STS894},
  eprint  = {2210.01948},
  archivePrefix = {arXiv},
  annote  = {Crossref record for the DOI (Statistical Science 38(4), November 2023);
             page range 576--601 from the Project Euclid article page, which Crossref
             does not carry. The arXiv v2 PDF (25 pp.) was read in full. The definitions
             this project relies on are all in its Section 2, verbatim: a test
             (super)martingale is a nonnegative (super)martingale with M_0 = 1 and is
             "the wealth process of a gambler who bets against P"; an e-process for a
             composite P is a nonnegative process dominated by a test martingale for
             every P in P, equivalently one whose value at every stopping time is an
             e-value; Ville's inequality is P(sup_t M_t >= a) <= 1/a, and its composite
             form sup_P P(exists t: E_t >= 1/alpha) <= alpha, which is what "converts
             e-processes into sequential tests". Section 1 gives the
             Forecaster/Skeptic/Reality protocol. Section 7 notes that testing whether
             probabilistic forecasters are CALIBRATED has been done game-theoretically
             with test supermartingales and e-processes -- the nearest existing object to
             the reading this paper's bridge paragraph offers, and the reason that
             paragraph claims the reading is available rather than new.}
}

